  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Instead of cash, Nagiyev gifts Kostya a bag of suspicious mushrooms from an office party and orders Ilya to bring him a festive meal—while in the kitchen Barinov wins a can-throwing bet.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "О, грибы! Мне один дурак на корпоративе дал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Крылья, говорит, прямо вырастают.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Будешь себя отлично чувствовать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Мне двадцать четыре.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты не умничай. Понял?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Илюша, приготовь мне что-нибудь праздничное.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сделай. Давай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "А вы что тут столпились?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Здравствуйте, шеф.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Опять ерундой занимаетесь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Шеф, это не ерунда, это большая игра.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Открытый чемпионат кухни по сбиванию банок. Солидные деньги на кону.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "О, грибы! Мне один дурак на корпоративе дал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Oh, mushrooms! Some fool gave them to me at an office party.' The meaning hinges on 'about' working together with 'to give' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Крылья, говорит, прямо вырастают.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wings, he says, sprout right out.' Notice how Russian expresses 'wings' and 'grow' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Будешь себя отлично чувствовать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You will feel great.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'will' before landing on 'to feel'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Мне двадцать четыре.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am twenty-four.' Ilya brings out the contrast between 'to me' and 'twenty' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты не умничай. Понял?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Don't be a smart-aleck. Got it?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'you' and finishing on 'understood'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Илюша, приготовь мне что-нибудь праздничное.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Ilyusha, prepare something festive for me.' The grammar packs 'Ilyusha' and 'festive' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сделай. Давай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Make it. Go on.' The meaning hinges on 'make' working together with 'come on' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "А вы что тут столпились?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And why have you crowded around here?' The plural past ending '-li' on the verb for 'crowded around' covers a group or the polite 'you'. Ilya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Здравствуйте, шеф.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello, Chef.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'hello' and finishing on 'hello'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Опять ерундой занимаетесь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you messing around with nonsense again?' Notice the reflexive suffix on the verb meaning 'are engaged in', turning the action back on the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Шеф, это не ерунда, это большая игра.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, this is not nonsense, this is a big game.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'Chef Barinov' before landing on 'game'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Открытый чемпионат кухни по сбиванию банок. Солидные деньги на кону.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The open kitchen can-knocking championship. Serious money is at stake.' Ilya brings out the contrast between 'open' and 'championship' in a few short words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
